==============================================================================
E7  CHOOSING m -- zero collisions is necessary, not sufficient
==============================================================================

  Exact-collision counts hit zero at every m tested, so the count alone cannot choose
  m -- a code that is merely non-identical can still be unlearnable. Margin decides.
  Sweeping m against the sinusoid `base` shows the inherited default is wrong: at
  base=10000 (the Transformer's, chosen for sequence positions in the thousands) the
  low-frequency channels barely turn across a 36-byte token, so they add shared mass
  and margin gets WORSE as m grows. Re-tuning base to the range byte positions
  actually span restores the expected behaviour.

  max adversarial cosine (lower = better separated); all cells have 0 collisions
    m  code_dim base=10      base=100     base=1000    base=10000  
    2       512     0.99002     0.99002     0.99002     0.99002
    4      1024     0.97373     0.98587     0.99546     0.99951
    8      2048     0.96443     0.98944     0.99536     0.99599
   16      4096     0.96270     0.99156     0.99627     0.99714

  At the inherited base=10000, margin degrades as m grows -- the opposite of
  what more dimensions should buy. At the calibrated base it improves with m.
  Chosen: m=8 (code_dim 2048, 16.6M params), best margin at m=16.

